#
# Two answers about background jobs that only zsh gives — #2318's zsh/ half.
#
# The first is what `$!` holds before anything has been backgrounded. bash,
# ksh93 and dash leave the parameter unset, so `${!-unset}` yields the default
# and a script can tell "no job yet" from "a job whose id is zero". zsh has it
# set to 0 from the start, and a script that writes `if [ -n "$!" ]` to mean
# "have I started one" gets yes on a shell that has started nothing.
#
# The second is `wait` with more than one operand. POSIX says the status is
# the *last* operand's, and bash, ksh93 and dash all report that; zsh reports
# the first. So `wait "$a" "$b"` is a different question here, and a script
# that waits for a batch and checks the status is reading a different job's.
#
# Nothing here prints a pid or a job number. `$!` is compared and used as an
# operand, never written out, which is the rule the core file states and the
# reason most of job control is unaskable in a suite with no expected output.

# `$!` before any background command. Printed through the `-` default, so what
# is compared is "set" against "unset" rather than a number.
printf 'before any job [%s]\n' "${!-unset}"

# After one, it is a number — asked as a comparison for the reason above.
( exit 0 ) &
printf 'after one job, positive [%s]\n' "$(( $! > 0 ))"
wait

# `wait` with two operands. The two jobs exit with different statuses, so
# which one is reported is the whole content of the case, and they are started
# in the order their statuses read.
( exit 3 ) & c=$!
( exit 4 ) & d=$!
wait "$c" "$d"
printf 'two operands -> %s\n' "$?"

# The other order, so a shell that always reported 3 for some reason unrelated
# to operand position would still be told apart.
( exit 4 ) & e=$!
( exit 3 ) & f=$!
wait "$e" "$f"
printf 'the other order -> %s\n' "$?"

# One operand is unambiguous everywhere, which is what makes the pair above a
# statement about operand *position* rather than about `wait`.
( exit 5 ) & g=$!
wait "$g"
printf 'one operand -> %s\n' "$?"
